@copilot Simplify the Kafka metadata fix in this PR. Update `open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-instrumentation:copilot/fix-kafka-consumer-metric-metadata-collection` directly, without opening another PR as a workaround. Keep the platform-assigned branch. Do not rename or replace it, and keep any follow-up branch GitHub assigns.

In `AbstractOpenTelemetryMetricsReporterTest.observeMetrics()`, replace the separate wait for `kafka.consumer.fetch_latency_avg` and `kafka.consumer.records_lag` with a metadata-aware wait that runs the existing post-consumption metric assertions. Both names are already in the expected metric set, so checking them separately duplicates coverage. Wait for the complete existing set and preserve the cluster ID and consistent point-attribute checks. Make sure a consumer data point carries a nonempty `kafka.cluster.id` before collection, rather than relying on a producer point to show the ID. Avoid a second raw `testing().metrics()` assertion pass, arbitrary metric names as readiness checks, and extra sleeps. Use the existing test helpers where possible. If the helpers cannot express a scope-wide list assertion, use the smallest test-only change needed to run it and preserve the existing collection behavior. Keep the javaagent and library meter scopes correct.

Keep this test-only. Do not change runtime code, enablement, or the generated catalog. Leave automated PR #21333 untouched. Apply the narrow Spotless formatter, exercise the registered Kafka javaagent `testMetrics` task with `-PcollectMetadata=true` and the shared test in the Kafka 2.6 library, and verify in the collected metadata that the consumer fetch latency, partition lag, and cluster ID are still present. Report any validation you cannot run.

@copilot Remove the new shared `waitAndAssertMetricsForInstrumentation` API from this PR. Update `open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-instrumentation:copilot/fix-kafka-consumer-metric-metadata-collection` directly, not a separate PR. Keep the platform-assigned branch without renaming or replacing it; retain any platform-assigned follow-up branch if GitHub creates one.

Use the existing `waitAndAssertMetrics(instrumentationName, metricName, assertion)` overload in a loop over `expectedMetricNames` in `AbstractOpenTelemetryMetricsReporterTest.observeMetrics()`. Put the metric point, nonempty cluster-ID, and consistent attribute-key checks into the existing helper's callback, with an explicit nonempty assertion so absent metrics or empty points cannot pass vacuously. This waits for the full expected set and records metadata using an established API. Do not retain special readiness checks for fetch latency or partition lag: those names are already expected. Preserve coverage for Kafka metrics and the correct javaagent/library meter scopes. Keep the change local to the Kafka test and remove this PR's additions to `InstrumentationTestRunner` and `InstrumentationExtension`. Do not add a replacement shared API, weaken existing checks, or add sleeps.

Keep runtime code, configuration, generated catalog files, and automated PR #21333 untouched. Apply narrow Spotless formatting and validate the javaagent Metrics suite with metadata collection and the shared test's Kafka 2.6 library execution. Confirm the collected consumer metadata still contains fetch latency, partition lag, and `kafka.cluster.id`. Report any blocked validation.
